; A `Map` carries no element type, and its reader must not pretend it
; does.
;
; `mapGet` answered `(-> Int Int a a)` until 2026-08-25. The type
; variable is in a PARAMETER - the default - so `AX3040` does not fire
; and nothing looked wrong. But `dflt` witnesses what the caller wants
; back when the key is ABSENT, and the cast inside `mapGet` sat on the
; FOUND path, where the word came out of the table. The two are
; unrelated, so the default silently chose the return type of a value it
; had nothing to do with:
;
;     (mapInsert m 1 100000000)
;     (strLen (mapGet m 1 "absent"))     ; check OK, exit 139
;
; Measured on the compiler before the change. It is the same shape
; `AX3040` refuses one level up - a type variable the CALLER chooses
; that the callee cannot have produced - reached through a default
; argument instead of through a bare result.
;
; `mapGet` answers `Int` now, so that program is a type error at
; `check`, and `mapGetStr` is the typed reader beside it. The pair is
; what `vecGet`/`vecGetStr` and `memGetWord`/`memGetWordStr` already
; were; `Map` was the last container whose reader wore a polymorphic
; spelling.
;
; THIS FIXTURE IS THE POSITIVE HALF. It pins that both readers work and
; that a `Map` really does hold either kind of word. The refusal itself
; is `tests/diagnostics/354-map-value-type.ax`, because a program
; that does not compile cannot be a golden that prints.
(import Map)

(import IO)

(import Fmt)

(import Str)

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main)
  (let (
    (ints mapNew)
    (strs mapNew)
  )
    {
      (mapInsert ints 1 100000000)
      (mapInsert strs 2 "interned")
      ; the raw reader answers the word, and the word is the Int
      (println (fmtInt (mapGet ints 1 (- 0 1))))
      ; the typed reader says at the CALL what the word is
      (println (mapGetStr strs 2 "absent"))
      ; and an absent key answers the default, on both
      (println (fmtInt (mapGet ints 99 (- 0 7))))
      (println (mapGetStr strs 99 "missing"))
      0
    }
  )
)
